WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.060
Why does fresh pineapple keep gelatin runny, while canned pineapple lets it set?

2
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.320
The first film gave us two dials: how many active sites are working, and how fast

3
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.820
each one works. Heat turns both of them.

4
00:00:18.783 --> 00:00:20.343
Gelatin is a protein.

5
00:00:21.603 --> 00:00:26.683
As it cools, its long chains link up into a net that traps water, and the dessert

6
00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:32.663
turns firm. Fresh pineapple contains bromelain, really a small family of

7
00:00:32.663 --> 00:00:33.923
protein-cutting enzymes.

8
00:00:35.423 --> 00:00:38.203
Bromelain cuts the chains, so the net cannot form.

9
00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:42.253
So the question is what heat does to bromelain.

10
00:00:43.253 --> 00:00:47.333
Hold equal tubes of it at different temperatures, each for the same time.

11
00:00:48.553 --> 00:00:50.953
Then compare both dials from tube to tube.

12
00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:57.153
Warmer molecules move faster, so more of them have enough energy to get over the

13
00:00:57.153 --> 00:00:58.433
activation-energy hill.

14
00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:01.553
The speed of each working site climbs.

15
00:01:02.893 --> 00:01:05.573
Up to a point, a warmer reaction runs faster.

16
00:01:06.617 --> 00:01:11.737
But a protein enzyme is a folded chain, held in shape by many weak attractions.

17
00:01:13.157 --> 00:01:15.557
Too much heat shakes those attractions apart.

18
00:01:16.977 --> 00:01:21.257
To denature is for a protein to lose the folded shape it needs to work.

19
00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.367
A denatured enzyme is not dead.

20
00:01:25.607 --> 00:01:28.547
It is a molecule whose active site no longer fits.

21
00:01:30.007 --> 00:01:33.367
So, in the hotter tubes, the working-sites counter starts to fall.

22
00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.270
Now multiply the two dials at each temperature.

23
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.450
At first the rising speed wins, and the rate climbs.

24
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.470
Then the falling counter wins, and the rate drops.

25
00:01:46.533 --> 00:01:51.033
The top of the curve is the optimum, the temperature at which the enzyme works

26
00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:57.493
fastest. Above the optimum, the rate often falls because sites are being lost faster

27
00:01:57.493 --> 00:01:58.733
than the rest speed up.

28
00:01:59.717 --> 00:02:00.997
Back to the pineapple.

29
00:02:02.457 --> 00:02:04.737
Canning heats the fruit enough to denature bromelain.

30
00:02:05.937 --> 00:02:07.397
The counter falls toward zero.

31
00:02:08.677 --> 00:02:13.717
Too little is left to cut the gelatin chains, so they link up into a net, and the

32
00:02:13.717 --> 00:02:14.717
gelatin sets.

33
00:02:15.517 --> 00:02:22.197
Now a harder one. Hold fresh pineapple juice as hot as canning does, long enough to

34
00:02:22.197 --> 00:02:23.877
denature most of its bromelain.

35
00:02:25.057 --> 00:02:26.837
Then cool it and add it to gelatin.

36
00:02:28.217 --> 00:02:29.317
Will the gelatin set?

37
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.040
It is likely to set.

38
00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.220
Cooling does not usually fold the enzyme back up.

39
00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:48.420
A few proteins can refold, but heat damage like this is often permanent, like a

40
00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:49.420
cooked egg white.

41
00:02:50.233 --> 00:02:54.013
Heat speeds up each working site, and it can take sites out of work.

42
00:02:55.313 --> 00:03:00.373
In this model, the rate peaks at the optimum, where faster sites are offset by lost

43
00:03:00.373 --> 00:03:05.793
working sites. Real optima can have other causes, and depend on the test.
